## Suppressed dead letters (#871, EVT-05)

Unit [`suppressed-dead-letters`](../migration/stage-1/suppressed-dead-letters/EXECUTION-RECORD.md) (scope [`871`](../docs/scopes/871-suppressed-dead-letters.md)); Pekko `ActorRef.scala:579-614, 716-724, 736-743`, `Actor.scala:46, 60, 123-129`.

```go
type DeadLetterSuppression interface{ DeadLetterSuppression() }                 // an application type opts in by declaring the method
type SuppressedDeadLetter struct{ Msg DeadLetterSuppression; Sender Handle; To ID } // To is always System.DeadLetters().ID()
type AllDeadLetters interface{ allDeadLetters() }                                // sealed: DeadLetter and SuppressedDeadLetter
```

- **Implemented.** A dead letter whose `Msg` implements `DeadLetterSuppression` is published as `SuppressedDeadLetter`, never as `DeadLetter`, in the one
  funnel `System.publish` (Pekko classifies in the dead-letter ref). It reaches the subscribers of `SuppressedDeadLetter` and of `AllDeadLetters`, and only the
  stream: the deprecated `SubscribeDeadLetters` hub and its counter see `DeadLetter` alone. `Sender` is never nil (an absent sender is `System.DeadLetters()`);
  `To` is `System.DeadLetters().ID()`, not the actor that did not take the message, as the oracle observed. A nil `Msg` is an ordinary `DeadLetter`.
  `PoisonPill` and `Terminated` carry the marker; `Kill` does not.
- **Divergence 5a, delivered (slice 2).** A `PoisonPill` is now published as `SuppressedDeadLetter(PoisonPill, sender, deadLetters)` when it finds a stopped
  actor (`postLane`), an actor whose stop was accepted, or a full bounded mailbox, and when it was still queued as the stop was accepted
  (`takePortDeadLocked`, after the user messages that were dead-lettered). The delivery outcome is unchanged: the `PoisonPill` is still not delivered.
- **Behavior change:** a `Terminated` that finds a full bounded mailbox is a `SuppressedDeadLetter` (sender: the watched subject, recipient `deadLetters`), no longer a `DeadLetter`.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["a site publishes a dead letter"] --> B["System.publish"]
    B --> C{"Msg implements DeadLetterSuppression?"}
    C -->|"no"| D["hub callbacks, then the stream: DeadLetter and AllDeadLetters"]
    C -->|"yes"| E["the stream only: SuppressedDeadLetter and AllDeadLetters, To = deadLetters"]
```

- **Evidence.** Pekko oracle [`suppressed-dead-letters-2026-11-10.md`](../docs/parity/oracles/suppressed-dead-letters-2026-11-10.md) (Java 1.8.0_504, 3 runs each):
  DL1, DL1b, DL2, DL3, DL4, DL5, DL7, DL8, DL8c are ported as Go tests
  ([`suppressed_deadletter_oracle_test.go`](actor/suppressed_deadletter_oracle_test.go), [`suppressed_deadletter_poison_test.go`](actor/suppressed_deadletter_poison_test.go)),
  and the spec `DeadLetterSupressionSpec.scala:41-72, :74-106` in [`suppressed_deadletter_spec_test.go`](actor/suppressed_deadletter_spec_test.go).
  The sender = watched subject of a `Terminated` into a full mailbox is **inferred from `DeathWatch.scala:111`**, not observed. Go unit evidence only
  (mutations in the record): no judge scenario and no differential run, so no V2 or V3 is claimed; the V level of row C-10b is the Owner's.
- **Not implemented, with markers:** the `ActorSelection` halves of the spec (`PORT-GAP(sel-871)`), `WrappedMessage` and the skip of the default listener (DL6, #1479),
  `Watch` and `Identify` on the sink (`PORT-GAP(dl-watch-896)`), `Terminate()` as a suppressed system message (no counterpart), `Dropped` (#906), `UnhandledMessage` (#897).

## Current scope

This is an initial local actor core. The exported `Ask` exists for local actors (see "Ask: request and reply");
`AskFrom`, `StatusReply`, shutdown of pending Asks, `CoordinatedShutdown`,
routing, remoting, clustering and persistence are not implemented;
deathwatch is implemented for local actors only (above), and so is hierarchical termination (above).
The event stream has registration, publication, unsubscription and the removal of a subscriber that stopped ("EventStream: registration, publication, unsubscription and termination").
The module imports no other goPekko module and has no `spi/` directory: it
will be created with the first real port that ADR 0008 requires.
Sender (#814, [ADR 0032](../docs/adr/0032-local-sender-envelope.md), partly accepted by the Owner on 2026-10-24):
`TellFrom(ctx, ref, msg, sender)` is `Tell` plus an optional sender `Handle`, and the actor reads it with
`Context.Sender() (Handle, bool)` while its `Receive` handles that message: `(h, true)` with the very `Handle` passed,
or `(nil, false)` when the message was sent with `Tell` or a nil sender. The sender travels in the queued envelope, so it
never leaks to another message and concurrent producers do not overwrite each other. `Tell` keeps its signature and
guarantees. The sender has the lifetime of Pekko's `currentMessage`: set when a `Receive` starts, cleared when it
succeeds, kept when it fails or is resumed, cleared after `PreRestart` and after `PostStop`. So `PreRestart` sees the
sender of the failed message, `PostStop` sees it after a failure and none after a successful message, and `PreStart`,
`PostRestart` and the new instance see none. While a parent handles the failure of a child, the failing child is the
sender (D5a, accepted by the Owner on 2026-10-28; `FaultHandling.scala:306`): the parent's decider reads it (the same
identity as the decider's `child` argument, also for a child that is no longer registered, whose failure is then
dropped), and so do the `PreRestart` that follows an escalation (with a `ChildFailure` as `FailedMessage`) and the
`PostStop` of a parent stopped after one. It is never the sender of the message the child was processing, nor one the
parent retained from its own earlier failed message. It lasts like any other current message: the next `Receive`
replaces it and `PostRestart` and the new instance see none. `OnTerminated`, the death-pact failure and a `WatchWith`
message run with the observed (terminated) actor as sender (D5b), as in Pekko (`DeathWatch.scala:110, 81-91`): a handle
that carries the ID of the observed incarnation and its owning System, built by the runtime when the delivery is taken.
It is not the `Handle` the user passed to `Watch`, which is never retained, and a recreated actor under the same name
never aliases it. `ReceivePort` starts with none, and the decider keeps the failing child as sender. Limits, all
recorded and none a parity claim:
- `(nil, false)` cannot tell a stale `Context` (kept after its hook returned) from an absent sender. The rule that a
  `Context` is valid only during the hook stays documented and **not enforced**; no enforcement is promised. Using a
  `Context` outside the authorized call, by retaining it or using it concurrently, is outside the contract of #814, and
  detecting that use is not promised.
- The sender of `Terminated` and of the `WatchWith` message has no `Tell` and no promise that it can be replied to;
  `System.Done(sender)` answers `ErrUnknownHandle` and a closed channel once the registry retired the actor (it carries
  no done channel). It is held until the next delivery replaces it, a success clears it, and a failure keeps it as
  Pekko's `currentMessage`.
- `DeadLetter` carries the sender (#896, "Dead letters: the sender and `deadLetters`"), and `System.DeadLetters()` exists. `Forward` (#854) is
  not implemented. Pekko's `sender()` returns `deadLetters` where `Context.Sender` returns `(nil, false)`: only the dead-letter view substitutes
  (ADR 0032 D3 stays; changing it is another decision, not taken).
- A `Ref` the runtime did not create receives a `TellFrom` through its own `Tell`, without the sender. The sender is
  not authenticated, not validated and is retained even if it already stopped.
Tests: [`sender_test.go`](actor/sender_test.go), [`sender_failure_test.go`](actor/sender_failure_test.go).
Passing Go tests does not establish behavioral parity with Pekko.

## Parity with Pekko v1.7.1 (partial)

The Go core is run through the same judge as Pekko (`judge/`, contract in
[docs/judge.md](../docs/judge.md)) on the fifteen scenarios of the Go gate (`judge/go-scope.json`). Result and limits are in
[docs/actor-core-parity.md](../docs/actor-core-parity.md); parity of the module is
**not** established. What is demonstrated, in both controlled and concurrent mode
unless noted:

- Messages from one sender are received in send order (S01, S02; S02 concurrent only).
- A child's `PreStart` runs before its first `Receive`, and `Receive` never overlaps a hook (S01).
